A||||5||burdell1||Aluminum Wheels
Z||000000||burdell1||04-16-99||10:58 AM||||Does anyone out there know the best way to clean aluminum wheels?  Mine have that plastic film partially on and partially off and dirt gets trapped up in there and won't come off.  I've heard gasoline works, but I don't want to use something like that unless I absolutely have to.
Z||000001||theogre||04-16-99||11:25 AM||||the foaming wheel cleaners work pretty well. it might soak in better if you do it fisrt one way, and after you finish, rotate the wheel 180 and try it again. That way it might run into the crevases it misses first time.<p>You're ultimate solution tho is to refinnish the wheel. the coating will only continue to get worse. 
Z||000002||PaUL||04-16-99||02:09 PM||||hey, Ive found the fastest way to clean aluminum wheels. This only applies to non chrome plated, non-clear coated wheels. <p>Depending on how dull your wheels look, if they are real dull, use a 600 grit sandpaper and wet sand the wheel. If not too dull, use a dishwashing green scrubpad and an abrasive cleaner like Comet. After you are done, the wheels will appear to have fine scrathes but the dullness will be gone.<p>Next use a polishing compound like Mothers or Eagle 1 and polish the fine scrathes away. This may take some time but its much faster than using the polishing compound alone. After you are finished, your wheels will be shiny like new.
Z||000003||burdell1||04-17-99||07:27 AM||||What's a easy (and cheap) way to get that plastic finish off the wheels?
Z||000004||theogre||04-17-99||03:13 PM||||there is no "easy" way. You need to dismount the tires before you even try this. some removers will damage the tires if you don't.<p>You need to cemically strip the entire wheel, and then refinish it. you can't take the clear off without taking off, or damaging, the color coat.<p>You can mask and powder coat the wheel youself if you want. Powder Coating equipement is not that expensive, and is available from Eastwood.  <A HREF="http://www.eastwoodco.com" TARGET=_blank>http://www.eastwoodco.com</A>  If you opt for this..... do it out side. if you absolutly must use your indoor oven to bake the wheel, OPEN ALL the windows!
Z||000005||PaUL||04-19-99||02:38 PM||||burdell1, you could try and wetsand the clear off with fine sandpaper. As I said before, this will leave fine scrathes but you can later polish these away.
